                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Fiber 4 g+</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Check label</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Check label</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Check label</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Read the superfood list with skepticism. A blend that names 50 ingredients in a scoop that weighs 40 to 60 g, most of it protein, cannot contain a meaningful amount of most of them. Proprietary blends, where only the total weight is listed, make that impossible to check. The ingredients that matter, protein, fiber and any listed vitamins and minerals, are the ones with numbers on the panel.
          </p>

          <p>
            That does not make all-in-ones pointless. They are convenient, the protein and fiber are real, and the vitamin panels on fortified products are real too. They are simply best understood as a fortified protein shake with a small greens bonus, not as a replacement for a varied diet. Our <a href="/best-greens-protein-powder/">best greens protein powder</a> guide looks at the greens side in more depth.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-meal-replacement" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. All-in-One Protein Powder vs Meal Replacement vs Separate Powders
          </h2>

          <p>
            The line between an all-in-one protein powder and a meal replacement is calories. Meal replacements are built to stand in for a meal, typically 300 to 400 calories with balanced carbs and fat. Most all-in-ones sit between 110 and 250 calories. Ka'Chava, at about 240 calories, sits at the boundary and works as a light breakfast or lunch, especially blended with milk, fruit or nut butter. Huel Complete Protein, at about 110 calories, is a protein shake. Our <a href="/best-meal-replacement-shake/">best meal replacement shake</a> guide covers the products built to replace a full meal.
          </p>

          <p>
            The other comparison is buying separately: a plain protein powder, a separate greens powder and a multivitamin. That usually costs less per day than a premium all-in-one, it lets you choose a better product in each category and it lets you change doses independently, more protein after training, no multivitamin if your diet is good. It is also three things to buy and remember.
          </p>

          <p>
            Which suits you depends on what you would actually do. If you would skip the greens powder and forget the multivitamin, an all-in-one that you drink every day beats three products you buy and ignore. If you are training hard and want 25 to 40 g of protein in a post-workout shake, a dedicated whey or plant protein is the better tool, and you can take a multivitamin with breakfast. Our <a href="/best-plant-based-protein-powder/">best plant-based protein powder</a> rankings and <a href="/best-whey-protein-isolate/">best whey protein isolate</a> picks cover the protein-first options.
          </p>

          <p>
            One more consideration for lifters: almost every all-in-one is plant-based, usually pea with rice or seeds. Blends like these are complete proteins, but per gram they deliver a little less leucine than whey. At 20 to 25 g of protein per serving that is fine for general health, but someone using the shake as their main post-training protein may want a larger serving or a whey-based shake on training days.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="replace-multivitamin" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Can an All-in-One Shake Replace a Multivitamin or Vegetables?
          </h2>

          <p>
            A fortified one can stand in for a multivitamin; none can replace vegetables. Ka'Chava, Vega's All-in-One and Huel Complete Protein list substantial amounts of many vitamins and minerals on the panel, and if you drink one daily you are getting much of what a basic multivitamin provides. Check the percent Daily Value column for the nutrients you care about. Products with greens but no fortification, such as Orgain Protein + Greens and Amazing Grass, do not replace a multivitamin.
          </p>

          <p>
            Taking both is usually unnecessary and occasionally unwise. Stacking a fortified shake with a multivitamin can push some nutrients, such as vitamin A, zinc or iron, toward or past their upper intake levels, particularly if you also eat fortified cereals. For most healthy adults this is not dangerous at typical doses, but there is no benefit to doubling up. Pick one source of fortification and check the totals.
          </p>

          <p>
            Greens powders are not vegetables. A scoop of dried, powdered greens contributes some vitamins, minerals and plant compounds, but in small amounts, and it lacks the volume, fiber structure and variety of a plate of vegetables. Research on the health benefits of vegetables comes from eating whole vegetables over years, not from powdered extracts. Treat the greens in an all-in-one as a modest bonus, and keep eating real vegetables.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where all-in-ones genuinely help is with fiber and protein, which many people under-eat. A shake with 20 to 25 g of protein and 6 to 8 g of fiber, such as Ka'Chava or Orgain 50 Superfoods, closes a real gap for someone who skips breakfast. Our <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">how much protein do I need</a> guide and <a href="/best-protein-powder-with-fiber/">best protein powder with fiber</a> guide show how far one shake gets you.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="worth-the-price" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Are All-in-One Protein Powders Worth the Price?
          </h2>

          <p>
            For protein alone, no. All-in-ones are among the most expensive ways to buy protein. Ka'Chava at roughly $4 to $4.70 per serving works out to about $4 to $4.70 per 25 g of protein, several times the cost of whey, which is often around a dollar per 25 g or less in larger tubs. Even the Orgain picks cost noticeably more per gram of protein than a plain plant protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            The fair comparison is against what the shake replaces. If an all-in-one replaces a protein powder, a greens powder at $1 or more per serving and a multivitamin, the gap narrows considerably. If it replaces a breakfast you would otherwise buy, it may be cheaper. If it replaces nothing and you take it on top of your usual supplements, it is an expensive protein shake.
          </p>

          <p>
            The simplest test: add up the daily cost of what you would buy separately, plain protein, greens and a multivitamin, and compare it with the all-in-one per serving. Use cost per 25 g of protein for the protein part, which our <a href="/tools/protein-cost-calculator/">protein cost calculator</a> works out, and see our sibling guide to the <a href="/best-bulk-protein-powder/">best bulk protein powder</a> for how cheap plain protein gets at scale.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our rule of thumb: pay for an all-in-one if the convenience means you will actually have a better breakfast or take the nutrients consistently. Do not pay for it to get protein, and do not pay extra for a longer superfood list, which mostly adds ingredient names rather than meaningful doses.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Are All-in-One Protein Powders Safe? Heavy Metals, Herbs and Who Should Be Careful
          </h2>

          <p>
            For most healthy adults, a reputable all-in-one at the labeled serving is a reasonable daily product. There are three things to watch, and they are more relevant here than for plain whey: heavy metals in plant ingredients, herbal and adaptogen extracts, and the interaction of high vitamin and mineral doses with medications.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Heavy metals</h3>

          <p>
            Plant proteins, greens, cacao and seaweed-type ingredients can all take up lead, cadmium and arsenic from soil and water, and all-in-ones combine many of them. Consumer Reports' 2025 testing found plant products generally higher than dairy, and named Huel Black Edition and Garden of Life Sport among the products of most concern; we did not pick either. Chocolate flavors tend to carry more than vanilla because cacao is itself a source. Prefer brands that publish batch heavy-metal results or describe independent testing, as Ka'Chava does.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Herbs, adaptogens and probiotics</h3>

          <p>
            Many superfood blends include herbs, adaptogens such as ashwagandha or maca, and mushroom extracts. Doses are usually small, but these ingredients are less studied in daily use than protein or vitamins, and some interact with medications or are not recommended in pregnancy. Probiotics are generally well tolerated, but people with weakened immune systems should ask their doctor first.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Vitamins, minerals and medications</h3>

          <p>
            Fortified shakes add vitamin K, which matters for people on warfarin, and iron, calcium and other minerals that can interact with some medications or conditions. If you take prescription medication, are pregnant or breastfeeding, have kidney disease or have a diagnosed condition, check an all-in-one with your doctor or a registered dietitian before making it daily. The same applies to children and teens: these products are formulated for adults. Anyone with a food allergy should read the allergen statement on the label image, since blends often include tree nuts, coconut, soy or seeds.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying-on-amazon" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Buying All-in-One Protein Powder on Amazon: Labels, Bags and Renamed Products
          </h2>

          <p>
            All-in-ones are harder to buy well on Amazon than plain protein, because their labels are longer, they are reformulated more often and premium prices attract resellers. A few checks are specific to this category.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Check who sells it</h3>

          <p>
            At $60 or more per bag, Ka'Chava and similar products are worth counterfeiting or reselling old stock. Prefer listings sold by Amazon or by the brand's own storefront, and be wary of a price far below the brand's own site. When it arrives, check the seal, the printing and the lot code. The brand's customer service can usually confirm whether a lot code is genuine.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Read the label image, not the bullets, especially after a rename</h3>

          <p>
            All-in-ones change formulas often, and the bullet copy is rarely updated. Vega One, for example, has been renamed and reformulated as Vega's All-in-One Shake, and old and new listings can coexist, with different protein, probiotic and vitamin figures. Look at the nutrition and supplement facts image for protein, sugar, fiber and the vitamin panel, and compare it with the bag you receive. If they differ, the bag is right.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Check dates, sizes and flavors</h3>

          <p>
            Probiotic counts and some vitamins degrade over time, so the best-by date matters more for these products than for plain whey. Check it on arrival. Watch size variations too: many listings bundle a 15-serving bag, a 30-serving bag and single-serve packets, and the price shown may be for a different size or flavor than the one in the photo.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Compare cost per serving and per 25 g of protein</h3>

          <p>
            Work out cost per serving and cost per 25 g of protein from the listing: divide the price by the servings, then divide by the protein per serving and multiply by 25. That shows how much you are paying for the extras. Subscribe & Save typically takes 5% off, and up to 15% when you have five or more subscriptions in one delivery, which makes a real difference on a $60 bag. Some brands also run their own subscriptions at similar discounts, so compare both.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            We ranked these powders on protein per serving, the completeness of the rest of the label (vitamin and mineral panel, greens, fiber and probiotics), sugar and sweeteners, transparency about contaminant testing and cost per serving and per 25 g of protein. We excluded Huel Black Edition and Garden of Life Sport because of Consumer Reports' 2025 heavy-metal findings. We did not lab-test or taste these products ourselves; figures come from labels and retailer listings at the time of writing, and taste and mixing notes are buyer reports. Thick plant shakes mix better in a blender or a vortex shaker; our sibling guide to the <a href="/best-protein-shaker-bottle/">best protein shaker bottle</a> covers that. Our <a href="/kachava-review/">Ka'Chava review</a> and <a href="/orgain-protein-powder-review/">Orgain review</a> go deeper on the top two.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying an all-in-one just for protein</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                All-in-ones cost several times more per 25 g of protein than plain whey or plant protein. If protein is the goal, buy a protein powder. Pay for an all-in-one only if you will actually use the vitamins, fiber and greens in place of other products.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Stacking a fortified shake with a multivitamin</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Fortified shakes like Ka'Chava and Huel Complete Protein already supply many vitamins and minerals. Adding a multivitamin and fortified foods can push iron, zinc or vitamin A toward upper limits. Choose one source of fortification and check the totals on both labels.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating powdered greens as vegetables</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A scoop of dried greens provides small amounts of nutrients but not the fiber, volume and variety of real vegetables. The evidence for vegetables comes from eating them whole. Treat the greens in an all-in-one as a bonus, not a replacement.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting a 50-ingredient superfood list</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 40 to 60 g scoop that is mostly protein cannot hold meaningful doses of dozens of superfoods, and proprietary blends hide the amounts. Judge the product on the numbers on the panel: protein, fiber, sugar and listed vitamins and minerals.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring medications and medical conditions</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Fortified shakes contain vitamin K, iron and other nutrients that can interact with drugs such as warfarin, and many include herbs. If you take medication, are pregnant or have kidney disease or another condition, check with your doctor or a registered dietitian first.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What is the best all-in-one protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  For most people, Ka'Chava is the best all-in-one protein powder: 25 g of plant protein, added vitamins and minerals, greens, 6 g of fiber and probiotics in about 240 calories, per the label at the time of writing. It costs roughly $4 to $4.70 per serving. Orgain Organic Protein + 50 Superfoods is the better value.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Can an all-in-one shake replace a meal?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Some can replace a light meal. Ka'Chava, at about 240 calories with protein, fiber and vitamins, works as a small breakfast or lunch, especially blended with milk or fruit. Most all-in-ones are 110 to 160 calories, which is a snack. For a full meal, a dedicated meal replacement with 300 to 400 calories is better.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Ka'Chava worth the money?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It is worth it if it replaces a protein powder, a greens powder and a multivitamin, or a breakfast you would otherwise buy. It is not worth it just for protein, since it costs several times more per 25 g of protein than whey. Compare its price per serving with the cost of buying those products separately.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Do I still need a multivitamin with an all-in-one protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Usually not if your all-in-one is fortified, such as Ka'Chava, Vega's All-in-One or Huel Complete Protein. Taking both can push some nutrients toward upper limits. Greens-only blends such as Orgain Protein + Greens are not fortified, so they do not replace a multivitamin. Ask your doctor if you have specific deficiencies.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What happened to Vega One?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Vega One appears to have been renamed and reformulated as Vega's Organic All-in-One Shake, and retailers list several old Vega One flavors as discontinued. Listings for both can still appear on Amazon, with different nutrition panels. Read the label image on the listing and the bag you receive, and treat the bag as the final word.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Are all-in-one protein powders safe for daily use?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  For most healthy adults, a reputable all-in-one at the labeled serving is reasonable daily. Choose brands that describe heavy-metal testing, because plant and greens ingredients can carry lead and cadmium. If you are pregnant, take medication or have a medical condition, ask your doctor or a registered dietitian before using one every day.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Are all-in-one protein powders good for weight loss?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  They can help because protein and fiber keep you fuller, and a 150 to 240 calorie shake can replace a higher-calorie breakfast. They do not burn fat on their own, and adding one on top of your usual meals adds calories. Fit it into your daily calorie and protein targets, and see our weight loss protein guide.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
